## Capping Linker Installation
Installation of the capping linker was probed on the example of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ (Table 1), which possesses “unsaturated” metal nodes, and therefore, satisfies the initial criteria necessary to perform this synthetic approach. The choice of capping linkers is based on the size of the pocket between metal nodes in a parent framework, where additional linkers can be installed. For instance, the capping linker, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (length $=15\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ), was chosen because of the $\mathrm{Th}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-}10$ topology to cap cylindrical pores with the $16\times$ $16\textup{\AA}$ channels (Figure 2). In this case, $80\%$ installation of the capping linker was achieved according to $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis by heating $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\mathrm{-Me}}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\mathrm{-}}10$ in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-NH}_{2}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figures S7–S9). The synthesized $\mathrm{Th}_{6}–\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(TPDC-NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ (here and throughout the manuscript the capping linker is designated in parentheses) is the first example of an actinide-containing MOF successfully utilized for postsynthetic capping linker installation demonstrating
