Heating of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ in the presence of $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}$ (guest) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-NH}_{2}$ led to simultaneous capping linker installation and actinide species incorporation inside the framework, resulting in the formation of a material with 52 $w t\%$ of Th according to ICP data. In comparison to the “empty” scaffold, simultaneous heating of the sample in the presence of a Th-containing guest slightly increased the actinide content by $62~\mathrm{mg/kg}$ of material (calculated based on the Th content in a MOF). To perform a comprehensive analysis of all known Th-containing systems to date, we estimated the Th-content $(\mathrm{Th\wt\%})$ ) in all reported Th-based MOF as a function of $1/d$ ( $\dot{\boldsymbol{d}}=$ framework structural density, Figure 3). Based on our calculations, $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\mathrm{-Me}}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}$ (TPDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ ) with incorporated $\mathrm{Th}^{4+}$ ions in the pores has the highest Th $w t\%$ with minimal structural density among all of the currently prepared monometallic thorium-containing MOFs. The next step in the postsynthetic modification of MOFs was derivatization of a capping organic linker $\mathrm{(H_{2}T P D C)}$ with diethoxyphosphorylurea (DEPU) groups for selective actinide binding. Thus, we prepared $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC}$ -DEPU (Scheme 1), which was shown to be a good candidate for uranium extraction from seawater.40
The length of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC}$ -DEPU $(15\mathrm{~\AA~})$ makes it suitable for postsynthetic installation in $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ , which has two pockets of different sizes (11 Å and $17\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , Figure 4) as previously shown by the Zhou group.50 By utilizing $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{Z}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{r}}_{6}.$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ , we performed sequential installation of two different capping linkers. The first capping linker, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-}$ DEPU, was introduced into the structure as an anchor to covalently bind $\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+}$ uranyl species. The remaining free equatorial positions between metal nodes can be occupied by a shorter linker, such as $\mathrm{NDC}^{2-}$ $\mathrm{(NDC^{2-}~=~}$ naphthalene-2,6- dicarboxylate, Scheme 1). For $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC}$ -DEPU installation, crystals of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{-}}8$ were heated in a DMF solution of the capping linker at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which resulted in $53\%$ linker installation. In the second step, we performed simultaneous guest inclusion and capping linker installation. For that, $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C}$ (TPDC-DEPU) was heated in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDC}$ (Figure 4) in the presence of uranyl acetate at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which resulted in $76\%$ installation of the second linker, $\mathrm{NDC}^{2-}$ , according to $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis (Figures S10 and S11). The simultaneous installation of the capping linker and incorporation of guest species $(\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+})$ resulted in a high amount of uranium $(44~\mathrm{~wt\%})$ immobilized in the pores of the material. After installation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC}$ -DEPU as a capping linker, we observed a relatively small amount of uranyl ions coordinated to the anchoring group $(<1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , which could be explained by spatial anchor separation and a relatively low content of phosphoryl urea groups impeding cation coordination. Despite that fact, overall actinide content is still comparable with the actinide content in the metal nodes of frameworks prepared by direct synthesis.
## Actinide incorporation through metal node extension
As the next strategy for actinide integration inside a rigid framework matrix, we explored the possibility of metal node extension. This strategy was previously reported for transition metals such as nickel or cobalt using $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs.56 To extend this approach, coordination of actinide ions to the MOF metal nodes was probed by heating the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}8$ framework in a DMF solution in the presence of $\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 d (Table 1). According to single-crystal X-ray diffraction data, such treatment resulted in preservation of parent MOF topology while the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -node underwent structural modification and formation of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{8}(M e_{2}B P D C)_{4}U_{0.87}O_{2.61}}$ $(\mathrm{Zr_{6}U_{0.87}–M e_{2}B P D C–8}$ , Figures 5 and S12–S14). As shown in Figure 5, each metal node contains eight uranyl groups (i.e., four on each side of the metal node). Presence of a uranyl unit was also clearly observed in the FTIR spectrum, where the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{U=O}}$ vibrational stretches at 913 and $86\dot{6}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are pronounced (Figure S13). XPS studies of the prepared bimetallic systems also demonstrate the presence of zirconium and uranium in $+4$ and $^{+6}$ oxidation states, respectively (Figure 5 and S14). Thus, the reported bimetallic framework, $\mathrm{Zr_{6}U_{0.87}–M e_{2}B P D C–8}$ , is the first example of a successful metal node extension through actinide integration. For further exploration of actinide behavior, we studied the possibility of coordination of the uranyl unit in $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ to create a bimetallic system
with metal nodes solely occupied by actinides. Indeed, thermal treatment of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\mathrm{-Me}}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\mathrm{-}}10$ in the presence of $\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 d resulted in formation of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{4}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ (Table 1, Figures 6 and S15–S17), in which thorium-based metal nodes are extended with $\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+}$ units. Remarkably, both $Z\mathrm{r-U}$ and Th-U metal node extension was confirmed by single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction, and therefore, implied single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformations. XPS studies also confirmed presence of $\mathrm{U}^{+6}$ and $\mathrm{Th}^{+4}$ in the sample (Figure S17), while FTIR spectroscopic analysis revealed appearance of $-\mathrm{U}=\mathrm{O}$ vibrational stretches at 913 and $866~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (Figure 6). Thus, $\mathrm{Th}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{4}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ is the first example of a bimetallic actinide-based framework prepared through metal node extension. The next question, which we tried to address in our studies, was the possibility of simultaneous capping linker installation and An-containing guest inclusion using bimetallic An-MOFs prepared through metal node extension. In this case, we successfully combined the three synthetic approaches discussed
previously in the manuscript: capping linker installation, metal node extension, and guest inclusion and thereby, further built structural complexity of the An-based systems proposed initially. As a precursor for our complex approach, we used $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{0.87}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}8$ possessing “unsaturated” metal nodes necessary to perform the first attempts in combination with the developed approaches. To cap the cylindrical pores ${17\times19}$ $\mathrm{\AA}$ , the $Z_{\mathrm{r_{6}U_{0.87}-M e_{2}B P D C}}$ was heated in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}$ $\mathrm{SDC}^{2-}=4{,}4^{,}$ -stilbenedicarboxylate, length $=14\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , Scheme 1) and $\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ , which resulted in formation of $Z_{\mathrm{{f}_{6}}}\mathrm{{U}_{0.87}\mathrm{-Me_{2}B P D C(S D C)}}$ with $50\%$ $\mathrm{SDC}^{2-}$ linker installation (Figures S18 and S19). The overall actinide content was found to be $19\mathrm{wt\%}$ based on the ICP data, which is almost twice higher in comparison with the parent framework.
