## Transmetallation
We have extended the MOF modularity for An integration by probing transmetallation reactions successfully applied before for transition metal incorporation. Inspired by the reported example of a molecular $Z\mathbf{r}$ -containing planar 15- membered macrocycle,62 where successful $Z\mathbf{r}$ -to-Th cation exchange was achieved upon heating in solution (Figure 7), we attempted to perform transmetallation reactions in MOFs. However, heating of the extended structures such as $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{Z}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{r}}_{6}.$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ in the presence of a thorium salt at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , even for as long as $14{\mathrm{~d~}}$ , did not result in any detectable structural change (Figure 7). This fact could be explained by a much higher flexibility of metal environment in complexes rather than that in MOFs,62 despite the previously mentioned successful utilization of a MOF matrix as chelating ligands to trap unusual coordination environment of metal ions.63,64 To shed light on such a drastic difference in the behavior of molecular species versus rigid frameworks, we have carried out theoretical calculations of energy of formation. For that, we used the truncated model, $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ , shown in Table 2. Density functional calculations (DFT) revealed that zirconium-to-actinide exchange is not favorable according to estimated energies of formation $(E_{\mathrm{f}})$ . The energies of formation for substitution of one zirconium or six zirconium in the metal node were estimated to be 9.70 and $62.78~\mathrm{eV}$ (in the case of thorium) and 7.21 and $45.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ (in the case of uranium), respectively. The obtained values of $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ for the metal-exchanged truncated model are shown in Table 2. Thus, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -to-An $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{An}}&{{}=}&{\mathrm{Th}}\end{array}$ , U) substitution is energetically unfavorable in extended structures such as MOFs compared to the reported molecular complex.
In contrast to $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ , which is robust towards cationic exchange, soaking of $\mathrm{U_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ possessing the same topology in the presence of $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}.$ even at room temperature, resulted in preparation of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}$ 8, the first example of successful actinide-to-actinide cation exchange in MOFs (Table 1). The U-to-Th substitution can be monitored visually since it is accompanied by a drastic color change from dark green to pristine white (Figures 8, S20, and S21). Based on the ICP data, we confirmed almost complete $(94\%)$ U-to-Th exchange (Figures 8). Since such exchange occurred through single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformation, we were able to monitor absence of detectable topological changes by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. Our attempts to achieve Th-to-U substitution using $\mathrm{Th}_{6}$ -TPDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-12}}$ were not successful (Figure S22) probably due to differences of metal node stability, which are currently under investigation.
To probe capping linker installation on the stable bimetallic MOFs, the $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ MOF was heated in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}$ (length $=14\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) to cap cylindrical pores with $12\times16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ channels at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ \mathrm{h}$ , which resulted in formation of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}(\mathrm{SDC})$ with $76\%$ $\mathrm{SDC}^{2-}$ linker installation (Table 1, Figures S23 and S24). To the best of our knowledge, $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(SDC)}$ is the first example of a bimetallic solely An-containing system utilized for successful capping linker installation. Further heating of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}$ in the presence of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}$ and $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ resulted in preparation of a material with an overall actinide content of $67\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . Simultaneous incorporation of guest species $(\mathrm{Th}^{4+})$ and capping linker installation inside transmetallated $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}$ resulted in preparation of a novel bi-actinide Th-based system with the highest $\mathrm{Th/U}$ content $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ among all Th-based frameworks known to date (Figure 3).
To estimate the efficiency of the proposed approach, we performed a comparison of the actinide amount included in each synthetic step. For the first step (direct synthesis), we achieved $40{-}50\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ of actinide in frameworks, which were used as precursors for our studies (Figure 1). Further increase of An $w t\%$ could be achieved through transmetallation or metal node extension.
## CONCLUSION
To summarize, we applied a sequential multi-step approach utilizing MOF modularity and versatility to:
(1) prepare the first examples of An-bimetallic MOFs through metal node extension and transmetallation. Both reactions include single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformations. This allowed for insights on the structural information of both MOF-precursors and prepared An-MOFs
Pos1 and pos3 refer to the one-atom substitution site at 1 and 3. Inset shows relaxed structure of the $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ cluster. The black, grey, pink, and white spheres represent $Z\mathrm{r}$ , C, O, and H, respectively. Sites 1 and 4 are equivalent. Sites 2, 3, 5, and 6 are equivalent.
and structurally supported the observed differences in chemical behavior of extended structures versus molecular species.
(2) achieve the first examples of $\mathrm{Th/U}$ or U-integrated systems, postsynthetically modified with a capping linker. We have also demonstrated that sequential installation of two capping linkers, one of which was modified with a selective site for actinide binding, can be performed by utilization of a Zr-based platform.
(3) test the possibility of simultaneous capping linker installation and An-containing guest inclusion in bimetallic (Zr-U and Th-U) and monometallic (Th) systems. (4) prepare a Th-containing framework possessing, to the best of our knowledge, the largest pore aperture and highest measured surface area known to date. (5) synthesize a framework with the highest Th $w t\%$ with minimal structural density reported to date.
The described findings were possible due to the synthesis of the first examples of An-containing frameworks with “unsaturated” metal nodes.
To conclude, we demonstrated the unprecedented role of framework modularity towards stepwise building of hierarchical complexity in An-MOFs, which is essential for fundamental understanding of the mechanisms involved in actinide integration inside extended structures, and thereby, formulate principles for more efficient nuclear waste management.
