Synthesis and characterization of $\mathbf{Th}_{6}–\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{BPDC}–\mathbf{10}$ . A mixture of $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}$ $(24~\mathrm{~mg,~}65~\mathrm{~\upmumol})$ , $\mathrm{H_{2}M e_{2}B P D C}$ $(4.3~\mathrm{\mg}$ , $16~\mathrm{\upmumol})$ , trifluoroacetic acid $(25~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , and DMF $(0.75~\mathrm{mL}$ ) were mixed in a 1- dram vial. The mixture was heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a hot plate for $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the colorless crystals of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}$ - $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{BPDC}\mathbf{-}10$ $\mathrm{6.0~mg}$ , $2.1\ \mathrm{\\upmumol}$ , yield: $65\%$ ) were collected by filtration and washed three times with DMF. IR (neat, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ : 2920, 2853, 1665, 1593, 1546, 1408, 1382, 1255, 1206, 1090, 1006, 910, 863, 777, 732, and 658 (Figure S2). The metal node and packing of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ are shown in Figure 1. The detailed description of the data collection and refinement details are given in the Supporting Information (SI), and Table S1 contains the crystallographic refinement data. As shown in Figure S1, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-}10$ matches the one simulated from single-crystal X-ray data. Moreover, PXRD studies were used to confirm crystallinity of bulk material (Figure S1). The thermal stability of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ was studied by thermogravimetric analysis, which demonstrated the rapid loss of solvent molecules at the $25\mathrm{-}125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ temperature range (Figure S1). The observed weight loss $(\sim32\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ) at this temperature range can be attributed to  removal of the non-coordinated solvent mixture of DMF and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , which is in good correlation with the residual electron density calculated from the single-crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray data. Furthermore, the samples were characterized by FTIR spectroscopy and gas sorption analysis as shown in Figure S2. Before gas sorption analysis, the as-synthesized MOF was washed with DMF and evacuated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Fitting the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherm to the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) equation resulted in a surface area of $741~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure S2).
Synthesis of and characterization of $\mathbf{U_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ . A mixture of $\mathrm{UCl}_{4}$ ( $12~\mathrm{\mg}$ , $32~\mathrm{\upmumol};$ , $\mathrm{H_{2}M e_{2}B P D C}$ $(4.3~\mathrm{~mg~}$ , $16~\mathrm{\upmumol})$ , trifluoroacetic acid $(25~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , and DMF $(0.29\mathrm{mL}$ ) were mixed in a 1/2- dram vial. The mixture was heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a hot plate for $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the green crystals of $\mathrm{U}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ ( $7.0\ \mathrm{mg},\ 2.4\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}$ , yield: $61\%$ were retained in the mother liquor. IR (neat, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ): 2930, 2862, 1574, 1498, 1409, 1378, 1253, 1195, 1090, 1062, 1006, 916, 865, 780, 673, and 657 (Figure S4). The obtained crystals were suitable for single-crystal X-ray analysis. The metal node and packing of $\mathrm{U_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ are shown in Figure 1. The detailed description for the data collection and refinement details are given in SI. Table S1 contains the crystallographic refinement data. As shown in Figure S3, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{U}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}8$ matches the one simulated from singlecrystal X-ray data. PXRD studies were also used to confirm crystallinity of bulk material (Figure S3). Thermal stability of $\mathrm{U}_{6}.$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ was studied by thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S3), which demonstrated a rapid weight loss $(\sim38~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) occurring at the $25{-}300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ temperature range.
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathbf{Th}_{6}$ -TPDC- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ -12. A mixture of $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}$ (32 mg, $86~\mathrm{\upmumol}\;$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-NH}_{2}$ ( $20~\mathrm{mg}$ , $60~\mathrm{\upmumol}$ ), acetic acid $(100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , and DMF $\mathrm{4~mL}$ ) were mixed in a 2-dram vial. The mixture was heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling, the crystals of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}$ -TPDC- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -12 (25 mg, $6.9\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ , yield: $69\%$ ) were collected by filtration and washed three times with DMF. IR (neat, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ): 3342, 2928, 1659, 1597, 1549, 1386, 1253, 1180, 1089, 864, 838, 780, 709, and 658 (Figure S6). The metal node and packing of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}12$ are shown in Figure 1. The detailed description for the data collection and refinement details are given in the SI, and Table S1 contains the crystallographic refinement data. As shown in Figure S5, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\mathrm{-TPDC}}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}12$ matches the one simulated based on the single-crystal X-ray analysis. PXRD studies were used to confirm crystallinity of bulk material (Figure S5). Thermal stability of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}12$ was studied by thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S5). The observed weight loss $({\sim}42\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ in could be attributed to removal of the non-coordinated solvent mixture of DMF and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ used for MOF synthesis, which is in good correlation with the residual electron density calculated from single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray data. Furthermore, the samples were characterized by FTIR and gas sorption analysis as shown in Figure S6. Before gas sorption analysis, the as-synthesized MOF was washed with DMF and evacuated at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ . Fitting the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherm to the BET equation resulted in a surface area of $880\ \mathrm{{\dot{m}}^{2}/g}$ (Figure S6).
Synthesis and characterization $\mathbf{Zr_{6}U_{0.87}}–\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{BPDC}–\mathbf{8}.$ . To perform metal node extension, $25\mathrm{\mg}$ of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ (PCN$\dot{7}00)^{50}$ was soaked in $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of the $0.25~\mathrm{M}$ uranyl acetate solution in DMF, and then kept in a pre-heated oven at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3{\mathrm{~d~}}$ . Yellow crystals of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}U_{0.87}–M e_{2}B P D C–8}$ were collected by filtration and washed three times with DMF. The metal node and crystal structure of $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{0.87}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}8$ are shown in Figure 5. The detailed description for the data collection and refinement details are given in SI, and Table S3 contains the crystallographic refinement data. As shown in Figure S12, the PXRD pattern of $Z\mathrm{r_{6}U_{0.87}–M e_{2}B P D C–8}$ matches the one simulated from the single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray data. Therefore, PXRD studies demonstrate preservation of framework integrity after the metal node extension (Figure S12). Before ICPAES analysis the prepared sample was thoroughly washed using a Soxhlet extraction for $3\mathrm{~d~}$ to remove possible residual salt. The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -toU metal ratio was determined based on the ICP-AES analysis. FTIR spectroscopy was employed to confirm presence of uranyl unit due to the presence of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{U=O}}$ vibrational stretches at 913 and $866~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (Figure S13). Thermal stability of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}U_{0.87}–M e_{2}B P D C–8}$ was studied using thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S12), which demonstrated a rapid weight loss occurring up to $320~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . XPS studies demonstrate presence of $Z\mathrm{r}^{+4}$ and $\mathrm{U}^{+6}$ in the sample, which is consistent with the single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray studies and FTIR spectroscopic data (Figures 5 and S14).
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathrm{\bfTh}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{4}{\bf-M e}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\bf-8}.$ To achieve metal node extension, $25~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ was soaked in $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.25{\mathrm{~M~}}$ uranyl acetate solution in DMF and kept in a pre-heated oven at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{~d~}$ . The obtained yellow crystals were collected by filtration and washed three times with fresh DMF, and were subjected to single-crystal X-ray analysis (Figure 6). Table S3 contains the crystallographic refinement data. As shown in Figure S17, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{4}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ matches one simulated from single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray data, and therefore, MOF integrity was preserved after metal node extension (Figure S15). The Th-to-U ratio was determined based on the ICP-AES analysis, but before the ICPAES analysis, the prepared sample was thoroughly washed using a Soxhlet extraction for $3\ \mathrm{~d~}$ to remove residual metal salts. Thermogravimetric analysis was used to study thermal stability of the presented samples (Figure S15), which demonstrated a rapid weight loss up to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . XPS studies confirmed presence of $\mathrm{U}^{+6}$ and $\mathrm{Th}^{+4}$ in the sample, which is consistent with the Th-node extension determined based on single-crystal X-ray analysis (Figure S17).
Transmetallation attempts for $\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ . To explore the possibility of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -to-Th transmetallation, the colorless crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ $20\ \mathrm{\mg}$ , $11\ \mathrm{\upmumol}.$ ) were heated in $2.0\ \mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}$ solution $\mathrm{C}=67~\mathrm{mM})$ in DMF at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $14{\mathrm{~d~}}$ . The obtained sample was characterized by single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction and spectroscopic studies, both of which did not reveal the presence of thorium in the MOF skeleton and in line with our estimated energy of formation (Table S4).
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathbf{Th}_{5.65}\mathbf{U}_{0.35}\mathbf{-Me}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{8},$ . The green crystals of $\mathrm{U_{6-Me_{2}B P D C-8}}$ were washed once with DMF and soaked in a $0.20\mathrm{mL}$ solution of $0.17\mathrm{MThCl}_{4}$ in DMF for $3\mathrm{d}$ at room temperature. After $3\mathrm{~d~}$ , the resulting colorless crystals of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ were collected by centrifugation and washed thoroughly three times with DMF. The obtained colorless crystals were suitable for single-crystal X-ray analysis (Figure 8). Table S4 contains the crystallographic refinement data. As shown in Figure S20, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35^{-}}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}8$ matches the one simulated from the single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray data. PXRD studies were used to confirm crystallinity of bulk material (Figure S20). The Th-to-U ratio was determined based on the ICP-AES analysis. Thermal stability of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ was studied using thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S20). The FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ is shown in Figure S21.
General Procedure of Capping linker installation. Compounds $\mathrm{Th}_{6}–\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(TPDC-NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ , $\mathrm{Zr_{6}}{\mathrm{-Me_{2}B P D C}}$ (TPDC-DEPU)(NDC) $\mathrm{(NDC^{2-}}$ $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ naphthalene-2,6-dicarboxylate), $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(SDC)}$ $\mathrm{\boldmath~\cal~S}\mathrm{\boldmath~D}\bar{\mathrm{\boldmath~C~}}^{2-}=4,4^{,}$ -stilbenecarboxylate), and $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{0.87}-$ $\mathrm{Me_{2}B P D C(S D C)}$ were synthesized by the capping linker installation in parent $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ , $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{-}8}$ ,50 $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ , and $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{0.87}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ , respectively, based on the modified literature procedure.50 Crystals of the parent MOFs were heated in a DMF solution of a corresponding capping linker at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Table 1). The obtained crystals were collected by filtration. The washing procedure includes thoroughly washing with hot DMF to remove the residual capping linker. Simultaneous incorporation of actinides as guests and capping linker installation was performed by heating $\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ or $\mathrm{ThCl}_{4}$ , the parent MOF, and the organic linker of interest in DMF at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
Digestion procedure. To study the composition of the prepared MOFs by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy, a solution of ${500}~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of DMSO- $d_{6}$ and $3~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of concentrated HCl was added to $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the material, followed by sonication until complete sample dissolution. The $\%$ of capping linker installation was calculated based on linker ratios found in the $^\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{H}}$ NMR spectra of the digested samples. The highest amount of capping linker can be calculated from geometrical analysis of parent MOF structure and length of capping linker as shown by Zhou and co-workers.53
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}–\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(TPDC-NH}_{2})$ . The $\mathrm{Th}_{6}–\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(TPDC-NH}_{2})$ framework was synthesized through installation of the capping linker, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-NH}_{2}$ , into parent $\mathrm{Th}_{6}.$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-}10$ . The crystals of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{\cdot}10$ $(20.0~\mathrm{\mg})$ , 6.28 $\mathsf{\mu}_{\mathrm{|\mu}}\mathrm{mol})$ ) were heated in $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of $15.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-NH}_{2}$ solution in DMF at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a pre-heated oven for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . To remove the residual capping linker, as-synthesized $\mathrm{Th}_{6}–\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC(TPDC-NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ ) was thoroughly washed with hot DMF. Based on $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis of the digested sample (Figure S7), we found that $80\%$ of the capping linker, TPDC- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{2-}$ , was installed. The installation of $\mathrm{T}\mathrm{\bar{P}D C-}\mathrm{\bar{N}H_{2}}^{2-}$ was also confirmed by the presence of corresponding to –NH and –CN stretches in the FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}.$ - $\mathrm{Me_{2}B P D C(T P D C\mathrm{-}N H_{2})}$ (Figure S8). PXRD studies were used to confirm preservation of framework integrity after capping linker installation (Figure S9).
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r_{6}}\mathbf{-M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}$ (TPDCDEPU)(NDC). The $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C}$ (TPDC-DEPU)(NDC) framework was synthesized through stepwise installation of two different capping linkers into parent $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{\cdot}M e_{2}B P D C{\cdot}8}$ . In the first step, crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-8}$ (15.0 mg, $7.96~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ ) were heated in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC-}$ DEPU $\mathrm{C}=30.0~\mathrm{mM}$ ) solution in DMF at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The obtained single crystals thoroughly washed with DMF were still suitable for single-crystal X-ray diffraction. The detailed description for the data collection and refinement details are given in Table S2. In the second step, obtained $Z_{\mathrm{{r}_{6}-M e_{2}B P D C(}}$ (TPDC-DEPU) was heated in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDC}$ solution $\mathrm{C}=30.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ ) in DMF at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C}$ (TPDC-DEPU)(NDC) framework was thoroughly washed with hot DMF to remove the residual capping linker(s). The composition of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}{-}M e_{2}B P D C}$ (TPDC-DEPU)(NDC) was determined based on $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy (Figure S10). The installation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ TPDC-DEPU and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDC}$ was found to be $53\%$ and $76\%$ , respectively. PXRD studies were used to confirm MOF integrity after capping linker installation (Figure S11).
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathrm{\bfZr_{6}U_{0.87}–M e_{2}B P D C(S D C)}$ . The $Z_{\mathrm{{f}_{6}\mathrm{{U}_{0.87}\mathrm{-Me_{2}\mathrm{{BPDC(SDC)}}}}}}$ framework was synthesized through the installation of the capping linker, $\mathrm{SDC}^{2-}$ , into parent $\mathrm{Zr_{6}U_{0.87}}.$ $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-}8$ . The crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{U}_{0.87}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC-}8$ $(25.0~\mathrm{mg}$ , 11.3 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ) were heated in the $10~\mathrm{mL}$ solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaC=8.0\mM)}$ in DMF at $75\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting solid was collected by centrifugation and washed thoroughly with hot DMF. Based on $\mathrm{^{1}\dot{H}}$ NMR spectroscopic studies (Figure S18), the $\mathrm{SDC}^{2-}$ capping linker installation was found to be $50\%$ . PXRD studies were used to confirm MOF integrity after capping linker installation (Figure S19).
Synthesis and characterization of $\mathbf{Th_{5.65}U_{0.35}–M e_{2}B P D C(S D C)}$ . The $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}(\mathrm{SDC})$ framework was synthesized through installation of a capping linker, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}$ , into parent Th5.65U0.35- $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-}8$ . The crystals of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}\mathrm{-}8$ $7.00~\mathrm{mg}$ , 2.54 $\mathsf{\mu}_{\mathrm{|\mu}}\mathrm{mol})$ ) were soaked in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SDC}$ ( $8.00\mathrm{mM}$ , 4 mL) at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting solid was collected by centrifugation, washed thoroughly with hot DMF. Based on $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopic analysis of the digested framework, $\mathrm{SDC}^{2-}$ installation was found to be $76\%$ (Figure S23). PXRD analysis confirmed preservation of MOF integrity after capping linker installation. As expected due to additional coordination of the capping linker, $\mathrm{SD}\bar{\mathrm{C}}^{2-}$ , the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Th}_{5.65}\mathrm{U}_{0.35}\mathrm{-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}(\mathrm{SDC})$ matches the one of $\mathrm{Th}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPDC}{-}10$ (Figure S24).
Physical measurements. FTIR spectra were obtained on a PerkinElmer Spectrum 100. NMR spectra were collected on Bruker Avance III-HD 300 and Bruker Avance I $\mathrm{~I~}400~\mathrm{MHz}$ NMR spectrometers. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra were referenced to natural abundance $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ signals and residual $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ signals of deuterated solvents, respectively. Powder X-ray diffraction patterns were recorded on a Rigaku Miniflex II diffractometer with accelerating voltage and current of $30~\mathrm{\kV}$ and $15~\mathrm{\mA}$ , respectively. Thermogravimetric analysis was performed on an SDT Q600 Thermogravimetric Analyzer using an alumina boat as a sample holder at a heating rate of $5~\mathrm{{^\circC/min}}$ . ICP-AES analysis was conducted using a Finnigan
ELEMENT XR double focusing magnetic sector field inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometer (SF-ICP-MS) with Ir and/or Rh as internal standards. A Micromist U-series nebulizer $(0.2~\mathrm{ml/min}$ , GE, Australia), quartz torch, and injector (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) were used for sample introduction. Sample gas flow was 1.08 $\mathrm{{mL}}/\mathrm{{min}}$ , and the forwarding power was $1250~\mathrm{W}$ . The samples were digested in Teflon vessels with nitric and hydrochloric acids and then heated at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . Gas sorption measurements were conducted on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 system. Oven-dried sample tubes equipped with a TranSeal™ (Micrometrics) were evacuated and tarred. Samples were transferred to the sample tube, which were then capped by a TranSeal™. Samples were heated to the appropriate temperatures as determined by TGA. The evacuated sample tubes were weighed again and the sample mass was determined by subtracting the mass of the previously tarred tube. $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms were measured using a liquid nitrogen bath $(77~\mathrm{K})$ . Ultra-high purity grade $(99.999\%$ purity) $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and He, oil-free valves and gas regulators were used for all free space corrections and measurements. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy measurements (XPS) were performed using a Kratos AXIS Ultra DLD XPS system with a monochromatic Al $\mathrm{K}_{\upalpha}$ source operated at $15\ \mathrm{keV}$ and $150\mathrm{W}$ and a hemispherical energy analyzer. Samples were placed in small powder pockets on the holder and analysis was performed at a pressure below $1\times10^{-9}$ mbar. High-resolution core level spectra were measured with a pass energy of $40~\mathrm{eV}$ , and analysis of the data was carried out using XPSPEAK41 software. The XPS experiments were performed while using an electron gun directed on the sample, for charge neutralization.
Theoretical calculations.
Calculations were performed using the VASP software67,68 with plane wave basis sets. Projector-augmented-wave (PAW) pseudopotentials69,70 of Zr, Th, U, C, O, and H were employed in which the number of electrons treated as valence is 12, 12, 14, 4, 6, and 1, respectively. The PAW potentials were taken from the VASP library. Calculations were performed with a plane wave energy cutoff of $520~\mathrm{eV}$ and $\Gamma$ -only k-point. Structure optimization was performed until the norm of the atomic forces is less than $0.025~\mathrm{e}\mathrm{\bar{V}}/\mathrm{\AA}$ . Two different levels of theory were explored. One was within a pure density-functional-theory with the Perdew-Burke-Erzernhof exchange-correlation functional71 and with a Van der Waals dispersion correction (denoted as PBE-D3). The other was within a hybrid Hartree-Fock/DFT with the B3LYP hybrid functional72 and also with a dispersion correction (denoted as B3LYP-D3). The Van der Waals interactions were taken into account using the dispersion formula of Grimme et al.73 with Becke-Johnson damping.74 In addition, in the PBE-D3 set, an on-site Coulomb interaction was added to the uranium f electrons to improve the electronic structure of these localized electrons within the $\mathrm{\DeltaDFT+U}$ formalism.75 Based on previous studies,76–82 a $U{-}J{=}4.0\mathrm{eV}$ was used.
First, we optimized the $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ structure. Table 2 shows the relaxed structure obtained with B3LYP-D3, however the structure obtained with PBE-D3 is nearly identical. Subsequently a Th or U atom was substituted for one of the $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms. For this one-atom substitution, there are two unique sites, pos1 (1) and pos3 (3) as shown in Table2. The formation energy for this one-atom substitution $(E_{f}\{1\})$ is calculated from the following total energies (equation 1):
$\bar{E_{f}\{1\}}=E_{t}\{\mathrm{MZr}_{5}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}+E_{t}\{\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\}-E_{t}\{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mathrm{H\bar{C}O}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}\}-E_{t}\{\mathrm{M}^{4+}\}=1\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{tat}}^{1/2}\mathrm{O}_{8}\}.$
Where $E_{t}\{\mathrm{M}^{4+}\}$ is the total energy of an isolated positive ion $(\mathbf{M}=$ Th or U). The image charge correction due to periodic boundaries has been taken into account by using the static dielectric constant of DMF at room temperature $\varepsilon=37.65$ .83 Furthermore, substitution of all the $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms with Th atoms or $\mathrm{~U~}$ atoms was also investigated. Similarly to Equation 1, the formation energy for this six-atom substitution $(E_{f}\{6\})$ is calculated from:
$\begin{array}{r}{E_{f}\{6\}=E_{t}\{\mathrm{M}_{6}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}\}+6E_{t}\{\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\}-E_{t}\{Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{8}\mathrm{O}_{8}\}-6E_{t}\{\mathrm{M}^{4+}\}=6E_{t}\{\mathrm{Gr}^{4+}\}=1.}\end{array}$
Table 2 summarizes the formation energies. The results show that substitutions with Th or $\mathrm{~U~}$ are not energetically favored.
